1. Backup Only Structure (Schema)

To take a backup of just the structure (without data):

<mysqldump -u root -p --no-data database_name > structure_backup.sql>

--no-data: Ensures only the schema (structure) is dumped, without the actual records.


2. Backup Only Data (Without Structure)

To take a backup of only the data (without structure):

<mysqldump -u root -p --no-create-info database_name > data_backup.sql>

--no-create-info: Dumps only the data, without table creation commands.

3. Backup Structure and Data

To take a full backup (both structure and data):

<mysqldump -u root -p database_name > full_backup.sql>

4. Backup a Specific Table

If you want to back up only a particular table:

<mysqldump -u root -p database_name table_name > table_backup.sql>

5. Backup All Databases

To back up all databases:

<mysqldump -u root -p --all-databases > all_databases_backup.sql>

6. Compressing the Backup

To save space, you can compress the backup:

<mysqldump -u root -p database_name | gzip > backup.sql.gz>


Restoring the Backup

To restore from a backup:


<mysql -u root -p database_name < backup.sql>

For a compressed backup:


<gunzip < backup.sql.gz | mysql -u root -p database_name>


Backup All MySQL Databases at Once

mysqldump -u root -p \
  --all-databases \
  --routines \
  --events \
  --single-transaction \
  --quick \
  --add-drop-database \
  > full_mysql_backup.sql


  | Option                 | Purpose                                                   |
| ---------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |
| `--all-databases`      | Backs up **all databases**                                |
| `--routines`           | Includes stored procedures/functions                      |
| `--events`             | Includes scheduled events                                 |
| `--single-transaction` | Ensures a consistent snapshot (no table locks for InnoDB) |
| `--quick`              | Dumps row-by-row (lower memory usage)                     |
| `--add-drop-database`  | Adds `DROP DATABASE IF EXISTS` before each                |


(Optional) Compress the Backup

mysqldump -u root -p --all-databases --routines --events --single-transaction --quick --add-drop-database | gzip > full_mysql_backup.sql.gz


Restore the Backup Later

If file is uncompressed:

<mysql -u root -p < full_mysql_backup.sql>

If file is compressed:

<gunzip < full_mysql_backup.sql.gz | mysql -u root -p>


Full Process: Backup and Restore to Different DB Name

Source Server (where the original database lives)

Step 1: Dump the Structure Only

mysqldump -u root -p \
  --no-data --routines --events \
  old_db_name > old_db_name_structure.sql


mysqldump -u root -p \
  --no-data \              # No data, only schema
  --routines \             # Include stored procedures/functions
  --events \               # Include scheduled events
  --verbose \              # Show which objects are being dumped
  old_db_name > old_db_name_structure.sql


Step 2: Dump the Data Only

mysqldump -u root -p \
  --no-create-info --single-transaction --quick \
  old_db_name > old_db_name_data.sql

mysqldump -u root -p \
  --no-create-info \         # Skip table structure; dump only data (INSERTs)
  --single-transaction \     # Take consistent snapshot (InnoDB only)
  --quick \                  # Stream rows one-by-one (low memory)
  --verbose \                # Print progress info (table names)
  old_db_name > old_db_name_data.sql


Step 3: (Optional) Check for USE old_db_name; in Dump
You can skip this step if you added --no-create-db in the dump.

Otherwise, open the SQL files and make sure they don’t switch to the old DB:

grep 'USE ' old_db_name_structure.sql
grep 'USE ' old_db_name_data.sql


If you find any USE \old_db_name`;`, replace it:

sed -i 's/`old_db_name`/`new_db_name`/g' old_db_name_structure.sql
sed -i 's/`old_db_name`/`new_db_name`/g' old_db_name_data.sql


Step 4: Create the New Database

mysql -u root -p

Then in the MySQL shell:

CREATE DATABASE new_db_name;
EXIT;


 Step 4: Restore the Structure

 mysql -u root -p new_db_name < old_db_name_structure.sql


 Step 5: Restore the Data

 mysql -u root -p new_db_name < old_db_name_data.sql
